---
// Side Projects: the page where building is the point. Each project uses the same
// mini-format (the constraint · the call I made · what I’d build next).
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import TapedImage from '../components/TapedImage.astro';
import StickyNote from '../components/StickyNote.astro';
import StatusBadge from '../components/StatusBadge.astro';
import Highlight from '../components/Highlight.astro';
import InkDivider from '../components/InkDivider.astro';
import FiveDayPath from '../components/diagrams/SideProjectsFiveDayPath.astro';
import StatusScale from '../components/diagrams/SideProjectsStatusScale.astro';
import RoomErd from '../components/diagrams/SideProjectsRoomErd.astro';
import ConflictCalendar from '../components/diagrams/SideProjectsConflictCalendar.astro';
import TestsChecklist from '../components/diagrams/SideProjectsTestsChecklist.astro';

const webp = (base: string) => [{ type: 'image/webp', srcset: `/images/${base}.webp` }];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Side projects · Autumn Willard"
  description="What Autumn Willard builds on her own time, from a Rails app made in 5 days to a senior capstone, and why building makes her a better product manager."
  active="side-projects"
>
  <div class="page">
    <section class="intro" aria-labelledby="sp-h">
      <h1 id="sp-h">Side projects</h1>
      <p class="intro__sub">I build things, so I know what I’m asking engineers for.</p>
      <p class="intro__lede">My day job is deciding what gets built. On my own time I build things myself, partly because it’s fun and partly because it keeps me honest about what “just add a button” actually costs.</p>
    </section>

    <InkDivider seed={81} />

    <!-- 01. HirePurpose -->
    <section class="sheet-section" id="hirepurpose" aria-labelledby="hp-h">
      <span class="margin-mark" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
      <div class="section-body">
        <div class="split split--photo">
          <div>
            <h2 id="hp-h">My dream company is hiring? Crap.</h2>
            <p class="proj__tag">A tale of wanting to impress while under great distress. How hard could it be to build a Rails app in 5 days?</p>
            <p class="proj__meta"><StatusBadge status="Personal project" size="sm" /> <span>Ruby on Rails · 5 days</span> <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/autumnwillard/HirePurpose">View the code on GitHub</a></p>
            <p>37signals posted a junior Rails developer role. I’d planned to learn Rails <em>eventually</em> and apply in a few years. I was working in .NET at the time, and my only previous run at Rails had ended in a pile of routing errors. Then the posting went up, and “eventually” turned into “this week.”</p>
            <p><strong>The constraint.</strong> Five days, close to zero Rails knowledge and an application window that was closing fast. I’d already spent days on the cover letter.</p>
          </div>
          <TapedImage
            src="/images/hirepurpose-sketch-560.jpg"
            sources={webp('hirepurpose-sketch-560')}
            sizes="(max-width: 760px) 200px, 240px"
            alt="Autumn’s pencil sketches of the HirePurpose screens on the back of a meeting handout."
            width={560}
            height={996}
            tilt={2.5}
            loading="eager"
            class="proj__sketch"
          />
        </div>

        <p><strong>The call I made.</strong> My first idea was a proficiency tracker for homeschooling families: state standards by grade level, with progress tracking for each kid. Good idea. Wrong deadline. I cut it and built something I could actually finish: a candidate tracker for hiring managers. It was relevant to the team I was applying to, small enough to ship and big enough to teach me real Rails. It also had room for some personality.</p>

        <div class="figure-wide"><FiveDayPath /></div>

        <div class="split split--art">
          <div>
            <p>From there, scope stayed tight on purpose:</p>
            <ul class="ink-list">
              <li><strong>One model.</strong> Candidates, and that’s it, so I could ship the core before the deadline.</li>
              <li><strong>Statuses that admit how hiring really works.</strong> I started with the usual “reviewing” and “phone interview.” Boring. Hiring is way more subjective than we like to admit, so the statuses describe how you <em>feel</em> about a candidate, all the way down to <Highlight>Hell no</Highlight>.</li>
              <li><strong>Status is the whole point, so it’s everywhere.</strong> You can change it from three different places, and the list sorts from most to least likely to hire, with “undecided” at the bottom.</li>
              <li><strong>No page reloads.</strong> Status updates happen in place, using Turbo and partials instead of a JavaScript framework.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="split__art"><StatusScale /></div>
        </div>

        <p>I sketched the screens on the back of a meeting handout. The deploy fought me harder than the code did: my Windows setup kept throwing a Docker error, so I finished on a friend’s borrowed Linux machine.</p>

        <p><strong>What shipped in 5 days:</strong> create, edit and delete for candidates, login and registration, live status updates, a responsive layout with dark mode and a production deploy on DigitalOcean. The app is offline for now while I revamp it, so the code is the place to look.</p>

        <TapedImage
          src="/images/side-projects-hirepurpose-screens-1040.jpg"
          sources={[{ type: 'image/webp', srcset: '/images/side-projects-hirepurpose-screens-1040.webp' }]}
          sizes="(max-width: 760px) 92vw, 600px"
          alt="Three phone screens of HirePurpose: the candidate list sorted by status, a candidate’s details and the edit form."
          width={1040}
          height={644}
          tilt={-1}
          tape="corners"
          class="proj__screens"
        />

        <p><strong>What I’d build next,</strong> in priority order:</p>
        <ol class="ink-steps">
          <li><strong>Multi-user support,</strong> so a whole hiring panel can evaluate candidates together. Hiring is a team sport, and right now this app has one player.</li>
          <li><strong>Evaluation scores</strong> for communication, technical skills and culture fit, so “Hell no” comes with reasons.</li>
          <li><strong>Easier attachments:</strong> add and remove files from the candidate page, with visible file names.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="someday">
          <StickyNote tilt={-2.2} color="yellow" class="someday__note">
            The proficiency tracker I cut is still in a sketch file somewhere. It’s on my “someday” list, which is a backlog with no due dates.
          </StickyNote>
          <TapedImage
            src="/images/side-projects-tracker-wireframe-480.jpg"
            sources={webp('side-projects-tracker-wireframe-480')}
            sizes="(max-width: 760px) 80vw, 300px"
            alt="Wireframe of a proficiency tracker for homeschooling families, an idea Autumn set aside."
            width={480}
            height={280}
            tilt={1.8}
            class="someday__img"
          />
        </div>

        <p><strong>Epilogue:</strong> I made it to the second round, one of only a handful of candidates. I didn’t get the job, but the project did its job. And I learned I really like Rails.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <InkDivider seed={86} flourish="arrow" />

    <!-- 02. Room Reserve -->
    <section class="sheet-section" id="room-reserve" aria-labelledby="rr-h">
      <span class="margin-mark" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
      <div class="section-body">
        <h2 id="rr-h">Trying to make a senior capstone that doesn’t suck</h2>
        <p class="proj__tag">I refused to build a Pokémon card inventory. I built a room reservation app instead.</p>
        <p class="proj__meta"><StatusBadge status="Senior capstone" size="sm" /> <span>.NET MVC · C# · SQL</span></p>

        <p>Every capstone needs a problem. I picked one with real edge cases: businesses that rent rooms by the hour for things like conferences, receptions and community gatherings. Hourly bookings are where the double-booking headaches live.</p>
        <p>The business problem was my assumption. I looked at how a few local businesses handled bookings, but I never talked to any of them. A real version of this would start with those conversations.</p>
        <p><strong>The constraint.</strong> A school project with no real users. That meant the requirements had to come from me, and the work had to prove itself through design and tests rather than adoption.</p>

        <div class="split split--art split--center">
          <p><strong>The call I made.</strong> I treated it like a product, not an assignment: requirements first, then a data model, then wireframes, then code, then tests. The core rule was simple. <Highlight>Two reservations can’t overlap in the same room.</Highlight> Everything else (rooms, customers, reports) is built around protecting that.</p>
          <div class="split__art"><ConflictCalendar /></div>
        </div>

        <ul class="ink-list">
          <li><strong>Data model before screens.</strong> Rooms, reservations and customers, with reservations built on a general “booking” type so other booking types could be added later.</li>
          <li><strong>Consistent screens.</strong> I designed the key screens in Figma with the same table layout and search placement everywhere, so learning one screen teaches you the rest.</li>
          <li><strong>Guardrails in the data.</strong> A room with reservations on it can’t be deleted.</li>
          <li><strong>Tests on the rules that matter.</strong> Seven unit tests cover the date, guest-count and overlap rules.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="pair">
          <figure class="pair__fig"><RoomErd /></figure>
          <figure class="pair__fig pair__fig--tests"><TestsChecklist /></figure>
        </div>

        <div class="split split--note">
          <p><strong>What I’d build next:</strong> recurring bookings. The tests don’t cover them yet, so they don’t count as done.</p>
          <StickyNote tilt={2} color="pink" size="sm" class="rr-note">
            Nobody was harmed in the making of this capstone. Several Pokémon cards remain uninventoried.
          </StickyNote>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <InkDivider seed={91} />

    <!-- 03. This site: built with AI, before/after, and how I ran it -->
    <section class="sheet-section" id="this-site" aria-labelledby="site-h">
      <span class="margin-mark" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
      <div class="section-body">
        <h2 id="site-h">This site is a side project too. Meta, I know.</h2>
        <p class="proj__meta"><StatusBadge status="Personal project · built with AI" size="sm" /> <span>About 3 days · Claude Code</span></p>
        <p class="proj__tag">I rebuilt this portfolio with AI. I didn’t write the code or draw the pictures. I did the part I’m actually selling: deciding what to build, setting the rules and saying “not yet” until it was right.</p>

        <div class="ba">
          <TapedImage
            src="/images/side-projects-site-before-desktop-960.jpg"
            sources={webp('side-projects-site-before-desktop-960')}
            sizes="(max-width: 760px) 92vw, 30rem"
            alt="Before: the old home page, a plain white layout with a cartoon portrait and a short intro."
            width={960} height={600} tilt={-1.4} tape="corners"
            caption="Before: clean, a little generic."
          />
          <TapedImage
            src="/images/side-projects-site-after-desktop-960.jpg"
            sources={webp('side-projects-site-after-desktop-960')}
            sizes="(max-width: 760px) 92vw, 30rem"
            alt="After: the new home page, a sketchbook on graph paper with a taped-in portrait, a sticky note and page-flag buttons."
            width={960} height={600} tilt={1.2}
            caption="After: a sketchbook, so I can show work I can’t screenshot."
          />
        </div>

        <h3 class="sub-h">How I ran it</h3>
        <ol class="ink-steps">
          <li><strong>Rules before code.</strong> The first thing I had AI write was a rulebook, not a page: my voice, what counts as a real number, what’s confidential and what the site should look like. Every agent read it before touching anything. When something drifted, I fixed the rule, not just the page.</li>
          <li><strong>Discovery, then decisions.</strong> An agent audited my old site and my notes and drafted what to feature. It came back with about 60 questions only I could answer. I answered them, and corrected the framing twice: I’m a product manager with technical depth, and this site shows how I think, not my job history. AI drafted the copy from my answers. I approved every page.</li>
          <li><strong>Options, not opinions.</strong> Three agents built three full design directions in parallel. I picked one, borrowed the best parts of another, then ran about eight rounds of feedback until the sticky notes stopped looking like UI cards painted yellow.</li>
          <li><strong>When feedback is hard to put into words, build a tool.</strong> I couldn’t describe the button I wanted, so I had AI build me a playground with presets and sliders. A copy review came back with 112 suggestions, so I had it build an approval page where I accepted, rejected or rewrote each one. That page is now a reusable skill I run on any writing.</li>
          <li><strong>Research before taste calls.</strong> For the page turn, an agent researched what makes a click feel good, prototyped a fix on a separate branch and filmed it frame by frame, so I could compare before I said yes.</li>
          <li><strong>Check everything.</strong> Every number on the site traces to a source. Automated checks run at six screen widths. Lighthouse scores 96 to 100 for performance and 100 for accessibility on every page. A blunt editor agent reviewed all the copy, and I made the call on every edit.</li>
        </ol>
        <StickyNote tilt={-1.6} color="blue" class="site__note">
          Working with AI felt a lot like working with a fast, eager engineering team. Clear constraints, short feedback loops and one person who owns the decisions. That was me.
        </StickyNote>
        <p><strong>Built with:</strong> Astro and Claude Code, hosted on Cloudflare Pages.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <InkDivider seed={96} flourish="arrow" />

    <!-- 04. Design work -->
    <section class="sheet-section" id="design" aria-labelledby="design-h">
      <span class="margin-mark" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
      <div class="section-body">
        <h2 id="design-h">A few things I designed for Tech Handoff clients</h2>
        <p class="sub-h">I owned design as well as product at Tech Handoff.</p>
        <p>Client pieces from Tech Handoff.</p>

        <ol class="strip" role="list">
          <li class="strip__item">
            <TapedImage
              src="/images/tech-handoff-truck-magnet-720.jpg"
              sources={webp('tech-handoff-truck-magnet-720')}
              sizes="(max-width: 760px) 92vw, 340px"
              alt="Truck magnet design for Schwab’s Hauling and Excavation, a Tech Handoff client, on a pickup truck mockup."
              width={720}
              height={257}
              tilt={-1.6}
            />
            <p><strong>Truck magnets nobody asked for.</strong> Schwab’s Hauling and Excavation, a snow-plow client, wanted a brand guide. I pitched truck magnets too, and they went for it.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="strip__item">
            <TapedImage
              src="/images/side-projects-gameday-sign-720.jpg"
              sources={webp('side-projects-gameday-sign-720')}
              sizes="(max-width: 760px) 92vw, 340px"
              alt="Barbershop sign concept styled like an ’80s concert poster."
              width={720}
              height={318}
              tilt={1.4}
            />
            <p><strong>A barbershop called Gameday, styled like an ’80s concert poster.</strong> Four signage concepts for a shop in Wyoming that mix classic barbershop details with retro rock-band promo style.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="strip__item">
            <TapedImage
              src="/images/side-projects-schwab-shirt-720.jpg"
              sources={webp('side-projects-schwab-shirt-720')}
              sizes="(max-width: 760px) 92vw, 340px"
              alt="Sweatshirt design for Schwab’s Hauling and Excavation, a Tech Handoff client, front and back."
              width={720}
              height={430}
              tilt={-1.2}
            />
            <p><strong>Merch for the crew.</strong> A sweatshirt and ballcap for Schwab’s, part of their branding package.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p><a class="text-link" href="/work/tech-handoff/">Read the Tech Handoff pivot →</a></p>

        <StickyNote kind="card" tilt={-0.6} class="offclock">
          <p class="offclock__head"><strong>Off the clock: art gallery flyers.</strong> <StatusBadge status="Volunteer work" size="sm" /></p>
          <p>I also designed a few flyers as a volunteer for a local artist’s special exhibit. Not client work, just a good cause and a fun brief.</p>
        </StickyNote>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .intro { padding: var(--s3) 0 var(--s3) var(--margin-col); }
  .intro__sub { font-family: var(--font-hand); font-size: var(--step-2); line-height: 1.2; color: var(--accent-ink); max-width: 30ch; }
  .intro__lede { font-size: var(--step-1); max-width: 58ch; }

  .section-body > p, .section-body .split p { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .proj__tag { font-size: var(--step-1); font-weight: 600; }
  .proj__meta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem 1rem; align-items: center; color: var(--ink-soft); font-weight: 700; }
  .sub-h { font-size: var(--step-1); font-weight: 800; color: var(--ink-soft); }

  /* text + art side by side; stacks on narrow screens */
  .split { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 20rem); gap: var(--s2) var(--s4); align-items: start; margin-block: var(--s1); }
  .split--photo { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 240px); }
  .split--center { align-items: center; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 30rem); }
  .split--note { align-items: center; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 13rem; }
  .split__art { padding-top: var(--s1); }
  .split__art--card { padding-top: var(--s3); }

  .figure-wide { margin: var(--s2) 0 var(--s3); max-width: 34rem; }
  .section-body :global(.proj__screens) { max-width: 600px; margin: var(--s3) 0 var(--s3) var(--s1); }

  .someday { display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 300px); gap: var(--s3); align-items: center; margin: var(--s3) 0; }
  .someday :global(.someday__note) { margin: 0; --note-size: 15rem; }

  .pair { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.4fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--s3); align-items: end; margin: var(--s3) 0; }
  .pair__fig { margin: 0; }

  .split :global(.rr-note) { margin: 0; }

  .strip { list-style: none; margin: var(--s3) 0 var(--s2); padding: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 0 var(--s3); }
  /* images share one row and captions start on the same line */
  .strip__item { display: grid; grid-row: span 2; grid-template-rows: subgrid; }
  .strip__item :global(.taped) { align-self: center; }
  .strip__item p { font-size: 1rem; margin-top: var(--s2); }

  .section-body :global(.offclock) { max-width: 36rem; }
  .offclock__head { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.2rem 0.8rem; align-items: center; }

  @media (max-width: 1000px) {
    .strip { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
    .split--center { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .split--center .split__art { max-width: 30rem; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .intro { padding-left: 0; }
    .split, .split--photo, .split--note { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .split :global(.proj__sketch) { max-width: 170px; margin-left: var(--s1); }
    .split__art { max-width: 20rem; padding-top: 0; }
    .split__art--card { padding-top: 0; }
    .someday { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--s2); }
    .someday :global(.someday__img) { max-width: 280px; margin-left: var(--s1); }
    .pair { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .strip { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .strip { row-gap: var(--s1); }
    .strip__item { max-width: 26rem; }
    .section-body :global(.proj__screens) { margin: var(--s3) 12px var(--s3) 4px; }
  }
  /* This site: before/after, side by side on wide screens */
  .ba { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--s2) var(--s3); align-items: start; margin: var(--s2) 0 var(--s3); max-width: 62rem; }
  .ba :global(figure) { margin: 0; }
  .section-body :global(.site__note) { max-width: 22rem; margin: var(--s2) 0; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .ba { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--s3); } }
</style>
